      "id": 1,
      "text": "Caledonia began as Goose River in the 1870s, named for its spot where the Goose River meets the Red River of the North.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/caledonia_north_dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Hudson's Bay Company made Caledonia a steamboat landing and trading post in the 1870s, sparking boom as river traffic and a stagecoach line to Winnipeg flowed through.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/caledonia_north_dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Goose River became the first county seat of Traill County in 1875, but the settlement was renamed Caledonia that August.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/caledonia_north_dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1895 Caledonia Bridge, a Pratt through truss over the Goose River, is the longest and oldest of its kind still in use in North Dakota.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/caledonia_north_dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A post office opened at Goose River on November 2, 1871, marking the community’s growing importance as a frontier waypoint.",
